// Practice quiz: Localhost and ports
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/linux-foundations/localhost-and-ports

::o1 what localhost means::A classmate on the same Wi-Fi opens http\://localhost\:3000 on their laptop. Which computer receives that request? {
=Their own laptop #Correct. localhost always names the computer the request is made on, also reached as 127.0.0.1.
~Your laptop, because you started the app #Not quite. localhost never reaches another computer, even on the same network.
~A public server named localhost #Not quite. localhost is not looked up on the internet; it is this computer.
~The Wi-Fi router #Not quite. A request to localhost never leaves the machine.
}

::o2 read the port::In http\://localhost\:5173/login, which part is the port? {
=5173 #Correct. The host is localhost, the port is 5173 and the path is /login.
~localhost #Not quite. localhost is the host, the computer the request goes to.
~/login #Not quite. /login is the path on that server.
~80 #Not quite. http uses port 80 only when the URL gives no port.
}

::o2 no port in the URL::Which port does http\://localhost/health use? {
=80, the default port for http #Correct. When an http URL gives no port, the default port 80 is used.
~8080 #Not quite. 8080 is common for development servers, but only when the URL names it.
~443 #Not quite. 443 is the default for https, not http.
~No port; localhost does not use ports #Not quite. Every request goes to some port; here it is the default.
}

::o3 connection refused::You open http\://localhost\:4000 and get connection refused. What does it mean? {
=No program on your computer is listening on port 4000 #Correct. The request reached your computer, but nothing was accepting connections on that port.
~Port 4000 is already in use #Not quite. A taken port gives address already in use when a second program tries to listen, not when you connect.
~Your internet connection is down #Not quite. localhost requests never leave your machine.
~The program on port 4000 rejected your password #Not quite. No program answered at all.
}

::o3 address already in use::A program already listens on port 3000. You start a second program on port 3000. What happens? {
=It fails with an error such as address already in use; stop the old program or choose a free port #Correct. Normally only one program at a time can listen on a given port.
~It fails with connection refused #Not quite. Connection refused means nothing is listening; here something already is.
~Both programs share the port and take turns #Not quite. Normally only one program at a time can listen on a port.
~The second program moves itself to port 80 #Not quite. It does not move; you choose the port.
}
